A||N||7||KRMFiero||prep before painting||||5||
Z||000000||KRMFiero||09-03-2001||08:44 PM||||I need to paint my 86 SE, I am keeping it same color (Red) but i want to change the ground effects and spoiler from Black to Red also...   What is the best way to sand? I have a few stress marks in my fasias - how do i get them out?  how far do i have to bring everything down to? should i remove my ground effects to sand and before painting or what? should i use special paper on GFX? or on spoiler?  When i go to paint show what should i buy? any paint stripping stuff work?  Whats the best primmer to use and how do i apply it?<BR>Kyle||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||09-04-2001||07:51 AM||||to get the stress cracks out of the paint, you have to sand it down to bare plastic. Where the paints in good condition you can just scratch it up thoughly with red scotchbrites. You can sand the worse spots with #180-#220, but finish any of that with #400. Use laquer Primer-Surfacer. You can find it in spray cans, but if you have a paint spray gun its cheaper to buy in quarts. Mix it 1/2 & 1/2 with laquer thinner. it dries fast, and it has to be sanded with #400 before painting. It fills all the tiny imperfections, if you go thru anywhere, reprime it. Try to keep primer on bumpers to minimum, because the thickness of the paint film affects whether or not it cracks easily again. Any peeling, flaking or powdering of clearcoat has to be removed at least down to base color. I prefer leaving all body parts on the car to paint with the exception of the rear wing and headlite covers.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=11878523&p=52990885"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||09-04-2001||09:16 AM||||Don't skimp on sanding. My Indy has cracked paint and the cracks go all the way down to the primer. I'm using 240 wet/dry and wet sanding it all off. It's hard work but it will be worth the effort.<P> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/Indy/6-21/pics/image006.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://cn308598-a.wall1.pa.home.com/fiero/Indy/6-21/pics/image016.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000003||IwannaIRM||09-04-2001||01:10 PM||||Now that Indy looks like what I did to my Fiero way back when.<P>When I repainted, PPG recommended using a flexing agent to help prevent future cracking.  My new(8 yrs old) paint hasn't cracked yet.  Clear coat has thinned in some places but that is the painters fault.<P>Other than that paint still looks great.<P>Anyone else put in a "flexxing agent"?  And no, it wasn't a hoax, real part # and labeled can.  Came with proper mixing instructions.<P><P>------------------<BR>AIM - SWags2933<P>84 Fiero SE (being modified)<BR>88 Formula||||reg||1
Z||000004||MinnGreenGT||09-04-2001||01:36 PM||||IRM- I had a "Flexing Agent" mixed into my repaint 6 years ago.  The only cracks I have are on the front Fascia from a friend hitting a curb with it.  I have some slight chipping around my windshied, but it's from the painter not removing the window trim/seal before painting- also his prep work was questionable.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngtsig3.jpg"> <BR>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000005||84Bill||09-04-2001||03:12 PM||||I was wondering about the flex agent myself. Most folks say it's a waste but by the looks of the paint on my car flex agent would have atleast prevented the deep cracking, I think. I dont know but I think I'll do some research on flex pros and cons just to educate myself.<P>Just a interesting observation.<BR>The cracks in my paint go all the way down to the primer and if I sand lightly enough it looks like even the primer has cracked.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||09-04-2001||07:18 PM||||Of course they want to sell you flex agent. Its expensive and they make more money. I havent used it in years and years and years, because cars i did with it didnt do anything different from cars without it. Yes it may crack if you run into stuff, so dont run into stuff, flex agent or not. The factory dont use paint on the bumpers, its a vinyl based coating just like on interior parts and cant be applied by a shop or individual. Thats one reason older cars especially, the bumpers are slightly differnt color than the rest of painted car.<P>so if you want to waste money on flex, Ok, but careful prep is all you need.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Shiner||09-05-2001||12:29 AM||||Flex agent acutally works it's way out of the paint after a fairly short amount of time.  Things like urethane bumpers tend to twist and contort when you install them.  The flex agent is only for reassembly.  <P>  Like the others have said, used 220 wet on heavily flaking or scatched areas.  Don't try to feather with it much.  Use 400 wet over the entire car, and ALWAYS use a sanding block wherever possible.  Stay away from rounded areas; it will dig small flat spots very quickly.  So make sure you dip that paper in the water frequently (for your health, among other things), and hit EVERYTHING down with Prep-sol.  You don't want to be sanding any wax into the existing paint.<P>  And have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
